                        ],
                        "form": "умла́утах"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e105a758-df57-3c72-9bfa-9268ee5b3563",
                        "entry_id": "688f5157-562d-3079-88e9-b1d009e13f65",
                        "glosses": [
                            "лингв. перегласовка коренного гласного звука, который под влиянием последующего суффиксального гласного становится гласным переднего ряда"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "215c7689-d148-4176-9f7f-4017b39d9535",
                                "sense_id": "e105a758-df57-3c72-9bfa-9268ee5b3563",
                                "text": "Наиболее характерным примером этого рода является германский умлаут (Umlaut). Умлаутом называется частичная или полная ассимиляция предыдущего гласного последующему, обычно коренного гласного гласному окончания (суффикса или флексии)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75de71f7-59e1-35ba-9473-29735ec1bca2",
                        "entry_id": "688f5157-562d-3079-88e9-b1d009e13f65",
                        "glosses": [
                            "лингв. буква с точками в некоторых германских, кельтских, а также уральских и алтайских языках"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7492ba2f-7f90-3254-b263-efc495d405cd",
                        "entry_id": "688f5157-562d-3079-88e9-b1d009e13f65",
                        "glosses": [
                            "лингв. диакритический знак, указывающий на фонетическое явление умлаута [1] гласных звуков в немецком и некоторых других языках"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "acfb501b-325d-4ac4-92f2-9b69d55e2113",
                        "source_entry_id": "688f5157-562d-3079-88e9-b1d009e13f65",
                        "source_sense_description": "перегласовка",
                        "target_lang_word": "umlaut",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "umlaut",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "59e7daa3-1880-3a29-ab07-491469db10d6",
                                        "entry_id": "a50144ce-e9d7-33ea-b716-f267d308bd2e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An assimilatory process whereby a vowel is pronounced more like a following vocoid that is separated by one or more consonants."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a2c7e612-f9f4-488a-a2af-69aa1e7bea36",
                                                "sense_id": "59e7daa3-1880-3a29-ab07-491469db10d6",
                                                "text": "In fits of concealed despair that went unnoticed even by those close enough to touch, Julien cursed the language of umlauts, eszetts, and gerunds."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b0a2288-f33b-4933-92a5-986afb2e8546",
                                                "sense_id": "59e7daa3-1880-3a29-ab07-491469db10d6",
                                                "text": "A tittle is more or less the same thing (the dot over an i, for instance), except that it can be traced back to Medieval Latin for a little mark over or under a letter, such as an accent ague or a cedilla. I don't know whether an umlaut is one or two tittles. Maybe it's a jot and a tittle side by side."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "12b4b925-48af-3006-b220-c4ae9642908e",
                                        "entry_id": "a50144ce-e9d7-33ea-b716-f267d308bd2e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The umlaut process (as above) that occurred historically in Germanic languages whereby back vowels became front vowels when followed by syllable containing a front vocoid (e.g. Germanic lūsiz > Old English lȳs(i) > Modern English lice)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8d052694-97c2-3bca-8233-7e57db496521",
                                        "entry_id": "a50144ce-e9d7-33ea-b716-f267d308bd2e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A vowel so assimilated."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20126613-fb47-38e0-b46d-48ef573c71ab",
                                        "entry_id": "a50144ce-e9d7-33ea-b716-f267d308bd2e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The diacritical mark ( ¨ ) placed over a vowel when it indicates a (rounded) front vowel"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "74c62cf1-9472-3bfa-a84a-3e8f4910ce08",
                                        "entry_id": "a50144ce-e9d7-33ea-b716-f267d308bd2e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of diaeresis."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "informal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0af6b42-bc49-4402-b105-851c8a4ef943",
                                                "sense_id": "74c62cf1-9472-3bfa-a84a-3e8f4910ce08",
                                                "text": "\"Naïve\" takes an umlaut because it is pronounced as two syllables."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79b90d01-180e-44ac-9352-115650f00e03",
                                                "sense_id": "74c62cf1-9472-3bfa-a84a-3e8f4910ce08",
                                                "text": "California, like several other states, prohibits the use of diacritical marks or accents on official documents. That means no tilde (~), no accent grave (`), no umlaut (¨) and certainly no cedilla (¸)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "umlaut",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6aae633d-951c-32e3-ac5a-f08710964fa0",
                                        "entry_id": "8f73197b-5d43-3f8f-8c5d-5488d350a248",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To place an umlaut over (a vowel)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c5862d9b-91a7-4a54-a56e-116b2f4fd6b6",
                                                "sense_id": "6aae633d-951c-32e3-ac5a-f08710964fa0",
                                                "text": "We kept some of the foreign alphabet symbols such as the accented and umlauted vowels and Greek letters and used the rest of the space for more esoteric linguistic symbols such as \"barred-l,\" and \"engma.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2c8248da-46a5-37dc-83b5-a8f86b3bdc99",
                                        "entry_id": "8f73197b-5d43-3f8f-8c5d-5488d350a248",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To modify (a word) so that an umlaut is required in it."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5224b553-75c8-4c43-8b2d-0d22cdddd305",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8248da-46a5-37dc-83b5-a8f86b3bdc99",
                                                "text": "an umlauting vowel"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "849aabb5-dedc-4ee9-95cf-75f6b6a252ab",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8248da-46a5-37dc-83b5-a8f86b3bdc99",
                                                "text": "These, together with (some) Romansh lects, belong to our West Rhaetian; the /ȫ/ also supported by two lects from the Ticino which by our criteria are NWIt, and by the partially umlauting lects of Lombardy which together with (some) Engadine lects belong to our East Rhaetian."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
